; nothing has a meaning in the engine by its name alone: an `iff` is used as two implications,
; an `or` split by `case`, a `false` at the end of an `assume` block gives `not`, and `def`
; defines with `=` or `iff` -- only for the symbols a trusted theory gave that role with
; `builtin` (propositional.kurt: `builtin iff equivalence, or disjunction, not negation, false falsum`,
; equality.kurt: `builtin = equality`). Before, a file's own `iff` was used as an equivalence
; (found 2026-10-09, after the same bug for `or`, doc/kurt-soundness.md §8.28).

; an `iff` of this file, without propositional.kurt: just a relation
infix iff 10 10
prop iff 0 1 2
prop A, B
use A iff B
use B
expect "ProofError"
    A

; ... and a file can't give itself the role
expect "EvalError" "can only be given by a theory that comes with Kurt"
    builtin iff equivalence

; `not` and `false` of this file: closing the block gives no `not A`
prefix not 18
prop not 0 1, false
prop C
use C implies false
assume C
    false
expect "ProofError"
    not C

; `def` with an `=` of this file
infix = 20 20
prop = 0
expect "EvalError" "needs the theory that gives `=` its meaning"
    def c = 1
